<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-problems-with-soap" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Problems with Soap</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Traditional soaps (i.e., anionic surfactants), as described in the
      previous section, come with a few inherent drawbacks.
      This is why we only use hand soap to wash our hands.
      Many of these problems are resolved with modern synthetic detergents and
      cleansers.
      One simple example of a problem with traditional soap is the following:
      try to lather (bubble) up with soap and tap water.
      You might find that instead of bubbling, you get a weak lather and a dull
      white film.
      This is known as soap scum.
      Why does it form?
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-unlike-synthetic-detergents-soap-forms-scum-in-hard-water">
    <title>Unlike Synthetic Detergents, Soap Forms Scum in Hard Water</title>

    <p>
      Tap water often contains dissolved minerals, specifically calcium ions (
      <m>\ce{Ca^{2+}}</m> ) and magnesium ions ( <m>\ce{Mg^{2+}}</m> ).
      We call this <term>hard water</term>.
      <idx><h>calcium</h></idx> <idx><h>magnesium</h></idx>
      <idx><h>hard water</h></idx>
    </p>

    <p>
      The <m>2+</m> means that they are highly attracted to negative charges
      <mdash/> including the negative heads of traditional soap molecules.
      Hence, soap molecules bind strongly to the charged minerals.
      Instead of dissolving in water to form micelles, the soap molecules just
      precipitate out as a solid, sticky curd called <term>soap scum</term>
      (made of <term>calcium carboxylate</term> ).
      <idx><h>soap scum</h></idx> <idx><h>calcium carboxylate</h></idx>
    </p>
    <!-- Figure of carboxylate binding to Ca2+ -->
    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Fill two small jars (call them jars A and B) with tap water.
          In jar B, dissolve a spoonful of Epsom salt (magnesium sulfate) to
          create artificial hard water.
          Add a few drops of liquid hand soap to both jars, cap them, and shake
          vigorously.
          What happens?
          Why?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          In jar A, clear, thick foam forms due to the formation of micelles.
          In jar B, the magnesium ions disrupt micelle formation, forming cloudy
          water with less foam.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <p>
      To resolve the problem of soap scum, chemists invented
      <term>synthetic detergents</term> by tweaking the hydrophilic head.
      Instead of the carboxylate, which tightly binds to ions in minerals,
      detergents often use <term>sulfonate</term> or <term>sulfate</term> heads.
      <idx><h>synthetic detergent</h></idx> <idx><h>sulfonate</h></idx>
      <idx><h>sulfate</h></idx>
    </p>

    <p>
      These heads do <em>not</em> tightly bind to calcium or magnesium ions.
      Thus, unlike traditional soap, these detergents remain soluble and lather
      freely, even in mineral-rich hard water.
    </p>
    <!-- Figure comparing traditional soap (forms scum) vs modern synthetic detergents (forms micelles) -->
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-traditional-soaps-are-highly-alkaline">
    <title>Traditional Soaps Are Highly Alkaline</title>

    <p>
      Traditional soaps are <term>alkaline</term> (the opposite of acidic),
      meaning they have a high pH level.
      pH is a measure of acidity.
      In normal circumstances, pH generally ranges from 0 (very acidic) to 7
      (neutral) to 14 (very basic).
      Soap sits at a pH of around 9 to 10.
      You can see the pH of some household objects in
      <xref ref="table-ph-levels-common-household-substances"/>.
      <idx><h>pH</h></idx> <idx><h>acidic</h></idx> <idx><h>alkaline</h></idx>
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-ph-levels-common-household-substances">
      <title>pH Levels of Common Household Substances</title>

      <tabular>
        <row header="yes">
          <cell>Substance</cell>
          <cell>pH</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Battery acid</cell>
          <cell>0</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Human skin mantle</cell>
          <cell>4.5 to 5.5</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Synthetic body wash</cell>
          <cell>5.5 to 6</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Water</cell>
          <cell>7</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>Bar soap</cell>
          <cell>9 to 10</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
      Our human skin maintains a slightly acidic surface barrier (pH about 4.5
      to 5.5) known as the <term>acid mantle</term>.
      High-pH bar soaps strip oils aggressively and disrupt the acid mantle,
      potentially damaging skin health.
      <idx><h>acid mantle</h></idx>
    </p>

    <p>
      Fortunately, modern synthetic cleansers, known as <term>syndets</term>,
      can be formulated at a skin-neutral pH to clean gently without disrupting
      the health of the skin barrier.
      <idx><h>syndet</h></idx>
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
